\title{Notes on formulating discrete-induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{These are the accompanying mathematical notes for the experimental research
project FDIP. FDIP is read “F-dip”, for discrete-induced physics: physics built on
a discrete setting, with geometry, time and matter induced from it. It is also a
coined acronym for “Formulating Discrete-Induced Physics.”
The continuum is widely used to model physical reality, but it has no physical
counterpart at quantum-spacetime scales: for physical interactions, length, time and
energy have no operational meaning below the Planck scale
\citet{Section 3.1}{hossenfelder2013minimal}. Continuum field theories inherently
suffer from ultraviolet and infrared catastrophes, where divergences and singularities
appear, a sign that the theory chosen to model the physical process fails at such
conditions \citek{wilson2022mathematicism}. Attempts to quantize gravity have brought
the difficulty into focus, since the singularities of general relativity and the
infinities of perturbation theory are what they must confront
\citet{Section 2}{carlip2001quantum}. Recognizing the continuum as an
extrapolation rather than an observation \citek{hossenfelder2013minimal}, lattice field
theory chose to keep the continuum theory as the object of interest, and to use the
lattice to compute and as a regulator, whose spacing sets the short-distance cutoff,
and with it the Planck-scale bounds on time and energy
\citet{Section 7}{wingate2025lattice}. We believe the proper mathematical tools to
develop, to face these challenges head on, are to make the theory fundamentally
discrete, and to see the finite structure as the object, with the continuum an emergent,
large-scale description. The notes
therefore build physics from finite structures, with exact algebra and no limits, and
ask whether geometry, time and matter can be induced from them, revealing new physics
insight near the Planck scale while agreeing with continuum physics at large scales.}

\p{Background independence is usually counted a desirable feature of a fundamental
theory, and Wilson argues that it is a hidden assumption rather than a property the
Standard Model possesses \citek{wilson2025background}. With Mach's principle in mind,
that is, that fundamental parameters of a physical theory may depend on the local
background, the notes keep their constructions compatible with a background-dependent
physics.

We develop the mathematics that may facilitate a physics theory, with the
bubble-universe picture as a mental model. For a bubble universe to last longer than
an instant, and to grow structured enough to develop anything, it needs symmetries
that admit sustainable and rich physics, and a specific bubble universe must “pick”
one set of such symmetries. Ideally most fundamental parameters should be determined
by the symmetries, leaving some free parameters to be fixed by the initial conditions.
Taking background dependence into account, describing a concrete physical process
also requires supplying the local background. What the mathematics has to make
possible is saying which of those a parameter came from, and that is why the notes
keep induced and supplied data apart.}

\p{Physics motivates the notes, but the interest and the focus are the mathematics
that answers the challenges: no physical theory is claimed, and where the notes
include a falsifiable proposal for one, it is an example of applying the tools
developed here. The organizing
hypothesis is that operations which continuum analysis presents analytically can
be recast as algebra on finite-dimensional objects; the notes test it on small
cases before relying on it, and a proposed translation carries its carrier, its
map and the property proved of it.}

